1
00:00:03,000 --> 00:00:15,800
Six months from now, somebody opens this project. It might be a colleague. It might be you,
having forgotten every decision you made. What's there to tell them what happened, and why?

2
00:00:15,800 --> 00:00:21,800
Here is what you will be able to do by the end of this video.

3
00:00:21,800 --> 00:00:37,000
Start with the gap. Code is very good at recording what the software does. It's almost
useless at recording what you tried and abandoned, what broke, what you deliberately left
out, and where you used an AI assistant.

4
00:00:37,000 --> 00:00:48,600
That gap is what documentation fills. And it's the difference between a project that can be
handed over cleanly and one that effectively can't be handed over at all.

5
00:00:48,600 --> 00:00:52,200
A format that works: Five fields for every change.

6
00:00:52,200 --> 00:01:08,200
The date, with the day of the week — it anchors the change in the project's history. What
changed, in plain language a non-developer could follow. Whether AI was involved. What
difficulties came up. And how each one was solved.

7
00:01:08,200 --> 00:01:19,800
Those last two are the ones people delete before sharing. They're also the most valuable. A
change list without difficulties reads like marketing. With them, it reads like engineering.

8
00:01:19,800 --> 00:01:34,600
Here's a real one. This change list ships inside the app itself, on the Settings screen,
rather than sitting in a file nobody opens. That's a small decision that signals the project
takes its own history seriously.

9
00:01:34,600 --> 00:01:48,200
And in code it's structured, typed data rather than loose text. Which means it can be
displayed in the interface, and it can never drift out of sync with the build it ships in.

10
00:01:48,200 --> 00:02:01,000
AI involvement, because it's new, it's increasingly expected, and most people get it wrong
in one of two directions. Either over-reporting every autocomplete, or saying nothing and
leaving a reviewer to guess.

11
00:02:01,000 --> 00:02:13,400
The healthy position is neither avoidance nor concealment. Use AI assistants, and use them
well. Then record where one helped, at what depth, and what you did to verify the result.

12
00:02:13,400 --> 00:02:28,600
A four-level scale is enough. None. Reference, where you used it like documentation.
Assisted, where it drafted something you then reworked. Generated, where the code was used
largely as-is — and there you should say what you checked.

13
00:02:28,600 --> 00:02:40,600
One rule to hold firmly: never commit AI-produced code you can't explain. If you couldn't
debug it at two in the morning without the assistant, you don't understand it yet.

14
00:02:40,600 --> 00:02:55,800
On difficulties — be specific. 'Had trouble with layout' helps nobody. 'Constraints looked
correct on one device and broke on another; fixed by grouping controls into stack views and
constraining the stack' is something a colleague can use.

15
00:02:55,800 --> 00:03:09,400
And record the decisions you deliberately did not take. Choosing JSON files over Core Data
is a real decision. Without a note, the next person just sees an absence and wonders whether
you knew.

16
00:03:09,400 --> 00:03:25,400
Here's a checklist to hold your own project against. Every entry has a real date. What
changed is in plain language. AI involvement is stated even when it's 'none'. Difficulties
are honest, not sanitised. Every difficulty has a matching solution.

17
00:03:25,400 --> 00:03:39,400
Decisions you didn't take are recorded. Known limitations live somewhere findable rather
than being implied by silence. Secrets are never in the change list. And the changelog ships
with the build, so it can't drift.

18
00:03:39,400 --> 00:03:41,900
Let's recap the key points.

19
00:03:41,900 --> 00:03:44,400
Here is your practice exercise.

20
00:03:44,400 --> 00:03:55,600
And that's the course. Seven modules taught you to build an iOS application. Testing proved
it works. And documentation is what makes that work survivable by somebody else.
